{
  "requirement": "For node reads, the SDK constructs a `Snapshot` from\nthe contents returned by `Domain::node_content` and their\n`NodeContents::fingerprint` under\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.fingerprint`, without another read.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct Snapshot<T> {\n    pub content: T,\n    pub fingerprint: Fingerprint,\n}\n\nimpl<T> Snapshot<T> {\n    pub fn new(content: T, fingerprint: Fingerprint) -> Self;\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl<T: Clone> Clone for Snapshot<T>;\nimpl<T: Debug> Debug for Snapshot<T>;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately cover `requirements.sdk.graph.snapshot`. I found no gaps.\n\n**Obligations and the tests that cover them**\n\n1. **A node read builds a `Snapshot` from the `Domain::node_content` result and its `fingerprint()`.**\n   - `graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[direct_reads_return_complete_unicode_contents_and_snapshots]` calls `read_snapshot`. It asserts that `snapshot.content.format()` equals the original text and that `snapshot.fingerprint` equals `fp(original)`. It covers an empty string and a large multi-byte Unicode string.\n   - `graph/validation_tests.rs#fn[snapshots_record_exact_reads_and_retain_distinct_versions]` asserts that `read_snapshot(...).fingerprint` is `fp(\"before\")`.\n   - `tests/contracts.rs#fn[identities_remain_stable_and_fingerprinting_uses_the_supplied_snapshot]` asserts that `snapshot.content.fingerprint()` equals `snapshot.fingerprint`, and that the fingerprint value is `\"before\"`.\n   - `src/sdk.rs#fn[requirement_edges_and_input_freshness_use_the_sdk]` checks that the fingerprints of retained contents match the expected `Fingerprint` scheme and digest.\n\n2. **No other read.**\n   - Both the `direct_reads…` test and the `snapshots_record…` test assert the exact read log. In the first, `reads == [1, 1]` for one `read_snapshot` plus one `read`. In the second, `reads == [1, 1, 1, 2]` covers the direct `node_content` call, `read`, `read_snapshot`, and a failed read of node 2. Together these show one `node_content` call per read.\n   - The `contracts.rs` test asserts `reads == [1]` after the content is changed. This shows the snapshot's fingerprint and content come from the original read, and calling `fingerprint()` or `format()` triggers no re-read.\n   - The `direct_reads…` and `contracts.rs` tests also change the underlying node after the read and show the snapshot still has the old content and fingerprint.\n\n3. **The struct shape and `Snapshot::new`.**\n   - `tests/contracts.rs#fn[callers_can_pair_content_with_its_fingerprint]` builds a `Snapshot` with `new` from outside the crate. It asserts both public fields. This also exercises `#[non_exhaustive]`, because an external caller has to use `new`.\n\n4. **`Clone` and `Debug` implementations.**\n   - No test calls `Clone` or `Debug` on `Snapshot`. The `Snapshot` struct in `graph.rs#struct[Snapshot]` gets both from `#[derive(Clone, Debug)]`. A derive has no conditional or transformation of its own, so the untested-obligation exemption applies. I did not treat this as a gap.\n\n5. **Agent tool paths.**\n   - `agent/tests.rs#fn[graph_tools_record_delivered_snapshots_and_prepared_pages_do_not_record_delivery]` and `#fn[agent_edge_tools_record_selections_without_validating_targets]` show that the recorded node observation carries the fingerprint of the content read.\n   - Those tests mainly serve other requirements. Even so, the content-to-fingerprint pairing is asserted there.\n\n**Cited requirement.** `requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.fingerprint` is cited context. The tests that apply it to snapshots are `snapshot.content.fingerprint() == snapshot.fingerprint` and the check that the retained fingerprint is unchanged when the node later changes.\n\nNo unacknowledged gaps remain, and no `{fixme}` was needed.",
